<section id="who-this-is-for">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Who this is for</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">Leaders who want the stakes to be real.</h2>
    <p>People who carry a lot of responsibility and rarely make time for themselves, who are generally fit &mdash; regular hikers, comfortable on their feet for long days &mdash; but who are not athletes or climbers. No technical background is needed; the guide and the equipment handle that part. Not everyone arrives free of a fear of heights, and that is fine if it is workable.</p>
    <p>It is not for the experienced alpinist &mdash; for them this is a warm-up. It is not a beginners&rsquo; trip and it is not a leisure holiday. If you want the leadership work of the other retreats with real physical stakes attached, and four days genuinely away from your phone, this is the one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-line"></div>

<section id="designed-for-leaders">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Why a mountain, for leadership</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">Real stakes, and time with people who carry the same weight.</h2>
    <p>Sustained physical and mental pressure &mdash; fatigue, altitude, exposure, the commitment of being clipped to a cable with a long drop beside you &mdash; brings out the same instincts you rely on as a leader: staying clear-headed when you are tired, managing your own fear, reading how the group is doing, knowing when to trust the guide and when to back yourself.</p>
    <p>There is a moment on the Breithorn where the last few metres to the summit cross are exposed enough that, alone, I have turned around &mdash; I wanted someone there with me. In a group you don&rsquo;t turn around; you climb it together. That is most of the lesson.</p>
    <p>The via ferrata makes it sharper. You are roped in beside the others and the guide, and for a couple of hours your progress depends on the group&rsquo;s judgment and communication &mdash; the same conditions as a high-stakes decision at work, without the email.</p>
    <p>The rest is the people. There is a lot of unstructured time &mdash; in the huts, on the breaks, on the sun deck &mdash; and the group is chosen so that time is worth something: senior leaders from different industries comparing notes on how they actually handle things. It is not a training. Nobody is teaching you a framework or walking you through a management book. You take part in those conversations as much or as little as you want; some people come simply to be among people who understand the load.</p>
    <p>Like the other retreats, you step back from the day-to-day and come home with one idea that changes how you lead. Here, you earn it on the plateau.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-line"></div>

<section id="what-to-expect">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Overview</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">A week in the mountains. Four days off the grid.</h2>
    <p>You fly into Munich and we drive you the rest of the way. Two nights easing in at Maria Alm, then four days and three nights on the plateau, moving hut to hut across the Stone Sea. A via ferrata ascent of the Persailhorn to finish, then back down to a hotel and a closing dinner.</p>
    <p>It is built for people who are generally fit &mdash; not athletes, and not climbers. No technical climbing background is needed. But the days are long and exposed at altitude, and you should expect to be taken well out of your comfort zone.</p>
    <p>A certified local mountain guide is with the group the entire time. Dirk Horn, who designed the trip and has known these mountains for fifty years, travels as a member of the group. The helmet, harness and carabiners for the via ferrata are provided and fitted for you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-line"></div>

<section id="why-this-place">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">A personal note</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">My connection to the Stone Sea</h2>
    <p>It started with my grandfather. He was an engineer who ran one of Berlin&rsquo;s railway stations, and in 1954 a railway pass would take him clear across Europe for a few cents. On one of those journeys he got off the train at a village called Maria Alm &mdash; then just a little church and the houses around it, no tourism &mdash; and fell in love with it. He brought the family back the next year, and the year after that.</p>
    <div class="stat-callout">
      <div class="fig">50</div>
      <div class="say">Years I have been going back to the same valley &mdash; since my father first brought me, just the two of us, when I was six. The same family has run the guesthouse we stay in for all of them, now in its third generation.</div>
    </div>
    <p>The mountains above the village are the Steinernes Meer &mdash; the Stone Sea. You climb around three thousand feet on one of a few routes and come out on a plateau that looks like another planet: bare limestone, ridged like waves, which is how it got the name. It has been a crossing for seven hundred years &mdash; in the plague years, people walked it between Austria and Germany depending on which side the sickness was on. It is a ten-hour walk from Maria Alm over to the K&ouml;nigssee, and once a year, in August, hundreds of people still make that trek together.</p>
    <p>My father first brought me up there in 1977, when I was six &mdash; just the two of us, in the autumn, when the huts were already closing for the winter. Those are some of my most cherished memories. I have kept going back every few years since, across two continents. We spent our honeymoon there.</p>
    <p>As I moved into my forties and fifties, the climb got harder. The ascent that once took me an hour and a quarter now takes two &mdash; I could go faster, but there is no point. And the exposed sections, the fixed rope in the wall where you don&rsquo;t strictly need equipment but you are glad to hold on, started to make my heart race in a way they hadn&rsquo;t. That turned out to be the interesting part: something real to work with, a way to get back in touch with yourself. It is not reckless and it is not foolish. But it asks enough of your attention to take you out of your comfort zone.</p>
    <p>People kept asking whether I could turn it into something they could do &mdash; in a small group, with the right people. This is that.</p>
  </div>
