<section id="itinerary">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow">The expedition</div><h2>The week, day by day.</h2></div>
    <div class="timeline cols-4">
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Arrival</div>
        <h3>Into the valley</h3>
        <p>We pick you up at Munich airport &mdash; about two and a half hours out. If you are coming from North America you land in the morning, so the first night is a comfortable place in the middle of Maria Alm. We eat together that evening; jet lag is built into the plan, so sleep if you need to.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Day 1</div>
        <h3>Easing in</h3>
        <p>A common breakfast, then a gentler hike of around 3,000 feet on less exposed ground &mdash; <em>einlaufen</em>, finding your legs and letting the altitude and the time zone settle. Last night in the valley, and a dinner together before we go up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Day 2</div>
        <h3>Up to the Riemannhaus</h3>
        <p>An early start &mdash; the ascent faces south and gets hot. A bus to the trailhead, then about three hours and 3,000 feet up to the Riemannhaus, the first night on the plateau. The afternoon is yours: the sun deck, or the Sommerstein just behind the hut, and a first look at the Sch&ouml;nfeldspitze.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Day 3</div>
        <h3>The Breithorn, and the traverse</h3>
        <p>Over the Breithorn &mdash; straightforward until a short exposed step near the top that needs your full attention, and that you do as a pair, not alone. Then the long rocky traverse to the Ingolst&auml;dter Haus: four hours stone to stone, 2,000&ndash;2,500 feet of up and down. Second night on the plateau.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Day 4</div>
        <h3>The long day</h3>
        <p>A dawn climb of the Gro&szlig;er Hundstod for the view over the whole range, then a hard ten-hour traverse &mdash; back past the Riemannhaus, over the Wei&szlig;bachscharte, down to the Peter-Wiesental-H&uuml;tte for the last night up high.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Day 5</div>
        <h3>The via ferrata</h3>
        <p>Helmet, harness and carabiners on. The Persailhorn south wall &mdash; about 1,200 feet straight up, graded C on the A&ndash;E scale, clipped to a fixed cable the whole way. Over the summit and down the north wall, back to the valley by mid-afternoon, and a hotel room of your own again.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Day 6</div>
        <h3>Optional: the Sch&ouml;nfeldspitze</h3>
        <p>For anyone with the legs for it: a 12-hour round of the Sch&ouml;nfeldspitze, the range&rsquo;s signature summit, leaving at 6 a.m. Everyone else takes Maria Alm at their own pace &mdash; the Natrun lift up to the Jufenalm, or a quiet day. A closing dinner and debrief that evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-month">Day 7</div>
        <h3>Down</h3>
        <p>Check out after breakfast. The shuttle takes you back to Munich; we book an airport hotel so you can rest and fly out the next morning. If you are staying on in the area, tell us and we will help you plan it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
