<section id="preparation">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow">Before you go</div><h2>You don&rsquo;t need to be an athlete. You do need to prepare.</h2></div>
    <div class="case-hook">
      <p>Enrolment includes a preparation program that starts about three months out and runs at your own pace. It is not a boot camp. It begins with a call &mdash; with Dirk, not a doctor &mdash; about what you can comfortably do today, and from there he builds you a plan: conditioning for sustained climbing at altitude, and nutrition to go with it. We check in along the way.</p>
      <p>Part of it is physical and part of it is your head. A workable fear of heights is normal and manageable with preparation &mdash; plenty of people, Dirk included, have to work for it. If heights are a hard no for you, this is not the trip, and we will tell you so.</p>
      <p>Think of it as a self-guided fitness program for leaders, with the mountain as the reason to actually do it.</p>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="{{ site.calendly }}">Ask about the preparation program</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-line"></div>

<section>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Equipment</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">What to bring, what we provide.</h2>
    <p>You bring two things that have to fit you personally:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a 30&ndash;35&nbsp;litre backpack &mdash; enough for four days on the plateau; we can recommend models</li>
      <li>proper hiking boots with a stiff sole for walking on rock &mdash; not trainers</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We provide and fit the technical kit for the via ferrata:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>helmet</li>
      <li>harness</li>
      <li>carabiners &mdash; a via ferrata lanyard set</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We buy this for you, and the owner of the local mountain shop comes in to size the helmet and harness in person. Afterwards it is yours &mdash; keep it for the next trip, or donate it to a local climbing club. The rest of the packing list comes with your preparation plan.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="dark-band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">Safety, and the guide</div>
    <h2>Serious country, run seriously.</h2>
    <p>A certified local mountain guide is with the group for the entire trip. Strictly speaking the terrain does not require one; having one means someone is always reading how the group is doing, adjusting the day to what the group can actually manage, and passing on the mountain-craft that only comes from living there. Dirk designed the trip and has known these mountains for fifty years, and he travels as a member of the group, not as the guide.</p>
    <p>For four days you are genuinely off the grid &mdash; no cell signal on the plateau. You are not on your own: mountain rescue, the Bergwacht, can reach the huts within about two hours. But between the huts you find your own way and deal with what the day brings. That is part of why people come.</p>
    <p>Dirk has walked this ground for fifty years and still feels the thrill every time &mdash; a section to cross, the weather, the length of a day. There is always something.</p>
    <a class="inline-link" href="{{ site.calendly }}">Talk to us about whether it is a fit</a>
  </div>
</section>

